From e91502a8d0b1d21ed547d35be117e5e201dadf60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 17:22:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] core, web: the Boot Config card saves through /api/command

Why: the card saved through its own route, POST /api/boot-config, with its
own lock, a revision header, status codes 400/403/409/500 and a rule that
asked whether the caller was a browser. Original Apertar has one door for
changes, handleCommand under commandMutex_, reached from the page by
sendCommand (fan mode: set_fan_mode, App set_fan_mode block), and a
read-only GET route for a file the browser shows (GET /api/config).

What: set_boot_config is only the handleCommand block now and the page
sends it with sendCommand like every other setter. The POST /api/boot-config
route, BootConfigReply, bootConfigMutex() and its three lock_guards, the
browser parameter and its old_page refusal are gone; bootConfigSet and
bootConfigGet return the reply body. GET /api/boot-config stays as the read
and takes no lock. The page reloads the card after any refused save, since
sendCommand hands it the sentence only. The reply fields are unchanged, and
the 400/409 codes stay in the body until the refusals are made one sentence.
README names the door.

Tested: check_core.sh both tests (builds, 21 host tests, no new warning).
--simulate with APERTAR_BOOT_CONFIG on a copy of the test fixture: POST
/api/boot-config -> Not found; set_boot_config over /api/command with
dry_run, with a stale revision (refused) and with the right one (one line
changes, config.txt.apertar.bak left); Chromium: change the cam0 sensor,
Save Only writes the one line. smoke_core shape diff: every base field
present with the same type. Needs the camera: Save from the card on the CM5,
reboot, journalctl -u apertar-core | grep 'boot config'.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NEFc1w7Y8dcyiV489BiwMr
